I"2G<p>Before you access the camera, contacts, and other privacy-sensitive data, you must request authorization. Otherwise, your app will crash when you try to access that private data. Xcode will log something like this:</p>
<blockquote>
  <p>This app has crashed because it attempted to access privacy-sensitive data without a usage description. The app’s Info.plist must contain an NSContactsUsageDescription key with a string value explaining to the user how the app uses this data.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Open the file named <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">info.plist</code> in your project, right-click and select <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">opening as Source Code</code>, then paste the code below into it. Alternatively, you can open <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">info.plist</code> with the default <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Property List</code> editor, click the add button, and when you type <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Privacy</code> — Xcode will give you autocomplete suggestions; use the up and down arrow keys to select.</p>

<p>The list of frameworks for private data is quite a big one:</p>
<blockquote>
  <p>Contacts Calendars Reminders Photos Bluetooth Sharing Headphones Camera Location Health HomeKit Media Library Motion CallKit Speech Recognition SiriKit TV Provider</p>
</blockquote>

